<% content_for :title, "Day 10 - Symbols vs Strings" %>

<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-10">
  <div>
    <%= link_to "← All questions", root_path, class: "text-sm text-pink-300 hover:text-pink-200" %>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-3">
      <span class="text-xs font-semibold px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full ring-1 bg-pink-500/15 text-pink-300 ring-pink-400/30">DAY 10</span>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Symbols vs Strings, memory basics</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-400">They can look almost the same. Under the hood, they're not.</p>
  </div>

  <%# ---------- Section 1: what's the difference ---------- %>
  <section class="rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-white/10 bg-white/[0.03] p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">1. What's the difference?</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
      <code class="text-pink-300">:name</code> is a <strong class="text-gray-200">Symbol</strong> - a fixed, permanent label.
      <code class="text-pink-300">"name"</code> is a <strong class="text-gray-200">String</strong> - real text data you can change.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2 rounded-lg bg-pink-500/10 ring-1 ring-pink-400/20 p-3 text-sm text-pink-100">
      <span>💡</span>
      <span><strong>Think of it like:</strong> a String is a piece of paper you can write on and erase. A Symbol is more like a barcode - fixed, and every scan of the same barcode points to the same thing.</span>
    </div>

    <%= terminal_window("symbol_vs_string.rb") do %>
:hello.class   # => Symbol
"hello".class  # => String
    <% end %>

    <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 ring-1 ring-emerald-400/20 p-4">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-emerald-300 mb-2">Live output</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1 font-mono text-sm text-emerald-200">
        <li>:hello.class =&gt; <%= @symbol_class_output %></li>
        <li>"hello".class =&gt; <%= @string_class_output %></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- Section 2: same symbol = same object ---------- %>
  <section class="rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-white/10 bg-white/[0.03] p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">2. Same symbol = the exact same object</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
      Every <code class="text-pink-300">:name</code> anywhere in your program points to the SAME object in memory.
      Every <code class="text-pink-300">"name"</code> creates a BRAND NEW object, even though it looks identical.
      <code class="text-pink-300">.object_id</code> proves it - it's like an object's unique fingerprint.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3 rounded-lg bg-white/5 ring-1 ring-white/10 p-3 text-sm text-gray-300 space-y-2">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">🔍 Step by step</p>
      <p><code class="text-gray-400">a = :name; b = :name</code> → both point at Ruby's one single :name object → <code class="text-pink-300">a.object_id == b.object_id</code> → <span class="text-emerald-300">true</span></p>
      <p><code class="text-gray-400">a = "name"; b = "name"</code> → each creates its own separate String object → <code class="text-pink-300">a.object_id == b.object_id</code> → <span class="text-emerald-300">false</span></p>
    </div>

    <%= terminal_window("object_ids.rb") do %>
a = :name
b = :name
a.object_id == b.object_id # => true  (literally the same object)

a = "name"
b = "name"
a.object_id == b.object_id # => false (two different objects)
    <% end %>

    <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 ring-1 ring-emerald-400/20 p-4">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-emerald-300 mb-2">Live output</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1 font-mono text-sm text-emerald-200">
        <li>:name.object_id == :name.object_id =&gt; <%= @same_symbol_ids_output %></li>
        <li>"name".object_id == "name".object_id =&gt; <%= @different_string_ids_output %></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- Section 3: mutability ---------- %>
  <section class="rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-white/10 bg-white/[0.03] p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">3. Strings can change. Symbols never can.</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
      Strings are <strong class="text-gray-200">mutable</strong> - you can change their contents in place, like with
      <code class="text-pink-300">&lt;&lt;</code>. Symbols are always <strong class="text-gray-200">frozen</strong> - there's
      no way to change one, ever.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2 rounded-lg bg-pink-500/10 ring-1 ring-pink-400/20 p-3 text-sm text-pink-100">
      <span>💡</span>
      <span><strong>Think of it like:</strong> back to the paper vs barcode analogy - you can scribble more onto the paper (String), but you can't edit a barcode (Symbol), only replace it entirely with a different one.</span>
    </div>

    <%= terminal_window("mutability.rb") do %>
str = "hello"
str << " world" # changes the SAME string object in place
str # => "hello world"

:hello.frozen? # => true  (symbols are always frozen, can't be changed)
    <% end %>

    <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 ring-1 ring-emerald-400/20 p-4">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-emerald-300 mb-2">Live output</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1 font-mono text-sm text-emerald-200">
        <li>str =&gt; <%= @mutated_string_output %></li>
        <li>:hello.frozen? =&gt; <%= @symbol_frozen_output %></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- Section 4: why it matters ---------- %>
  <section class="rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-white/10 bg-white/[0.03] p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">4. Why this matters: hash keys</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
      Since every <code class="text-pink-300">:key</code> is the exact same reused object, comparing Symbol keys is
      faster and uses less memory than comparing separate String objects that just look the same. That's why you'll
      see Symbols used as hash keys constantly in Ruby and Rails code.
    </p>

    <%= terminal_window("symbol_keys.rb") do %>
person = { name: "Ali", age: 25 }
# same as writing { :name => "Ali", :age => 25 }

person[:name] # => "Ali"
    <% end %>

    <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 ring-1 ring-emerald-400/20 p-4">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-emerald-300 mb-2">Live output</p>
      <p class="font-mono text-sm text-emerald-200">person =&gt; <%= @hash_output %></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
